[Term]
id: MP:0002356
name: abnormal spleen red pulp morphology
def: "any structural anomaly of the parenchymatous tissue network of the spleen that consists of loose plates or cords (sinuses) infiltrated with red blood cells where most of the blood filtration occurs and degenerate erythrocytes are removed from the circulation" [https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3691-0324]
synonym: "abnormal splenic red pulp morphology" EXACT []
is_a: MP:0000689 ! abnormal spleen morphology

[Term]
id: MP:0002357
name: abnormal spleen white pulp morphology
def: "any structural anomaly of the parenchymatous tissue of the spleen that surrounds splenic blood vessels, consists of compact masses of lymphatic cells and is where foreign material removed from the blood is used to initiate an immune reaction that results in the production of antibodies" [https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3691-0324]
comment: Note that there is no consensus in the literature about which compartments constitute the white pulp or the ramifications of the microvasculature. In addition, notable differences exist between humans and rodents. In this ontology, the marginal sinus and marginal zone have been included as part of the white pulp.
synonym: "abnormal splenic white pulp morphology" EXACT []
is_a: MP:0000689 ! abnormal spleen morphology

[Term]
id: MP:0002358
