[Term]
id: MP:0031112
name: rhabdomyolysis
def: "breakdown of skeletal muscle fibers leading to leakage of muscle contents into the bloodstream; damage to the sarcolemma results in loss of its intrinsic function, an influx of calcium, activation of intracellular proteases, and release of intracellular contents (e.g. myoglobin, creatine phosphokinase, aldolase, lactate dehydrogenase, aspartate transaminase, and potassium) into the circulation; common causes include trauma, prolonged exertion, hyperthermia, infections, electrolyte abnormalities, electrical current injury, drugs or toxins, and conditions that cause tissue hypoxia; complications may include acute renal failure, metabolic abnormalities, and disseminated intravascular congestion" [https://www.aafp.org/afp/2002/0301/p907.html, ISBN:978-1-4160-2971-7]
is_a: MP:0000759 ! abnormal skeletal muscle morphology
property_value: dc:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6490-7723
property_value: dc:date "2020-07-07T10:04:24Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: MP:0031113
name: pulmonary parenchymal consolidation
def: "replacement of alveolar air by fluid (exudate/transudate/blood), cells (inflammatory), tissue, or other material, rendering the pulmonary parenchyma airless; consolidated tissue is more radio-opaque than normally aerated lung parenchyma, so that it is clearly demonstrable in radiography and on CT scans" [doi:10.1097/CPM.0b013e3182a2b140, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pulmonary_consolidation#Radiology, https://radiologykey.com/consolidation-and-atelectasis/, https://radiopaedia.org/articles/lobar-consolidation]
synonym: "lung parenchymal consolidation" EXACT []
synonym: "pulmonary consolidation" BROAD []
is_a: MP:0001175 ! abnormal lung morphology
property_value: dc:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6490-7723
property_value: dc:date "2020-07-07T10:54:12Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: MP:0031114
name: increased circulating gamma-glutamyl transferase level
def: "elevated blood level of the enzyme that catalyzes the transfer of gamma-glutamyl functional groups from molecules such as glutathione to an acceptor that may be an amino acid, a peptide or water (forming glutamate); GGT plays a key role in the gamma-glutamyl cycle, a pathway for the synthesis and degradation of glutathione as well as drug and xenobiotic detoxification; GGT is present in many organs throughout the body, with the highest concentrations found in liver, and elevated in the blood in most diseases that cause damage to the liver or bile ducts" [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamma-glutamyltransferase, https://labtestsonline.org/tests/gamma-glutamyl-transferase-ggt]
synonym: "increased circulating gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase level" EXACT []
synonym: "increased circulating gamma-GT level" EXACT []
synonym: "increased circulating GGT level" EXACT []
is_a: MP:0014450 ! increased circulating enzyme level
property_value: dc:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6490-7723
property_value: dc:date "2020-07-07T11:27:05Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: MP:0031115
name: macrovesicular hepatic steatosis
def: "a common form of hepatic steatosis characterized by the presence of large lipid droplets that displace the nucleus and organelles to the cell periphery in affected hepatocytes; may be caused by oversupply of lipids due to obesity, obstructive sleep apnea, insulin resistance, or alcoholism" [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steatosis, ISBN:978-0-323-37591-7]
synonym: "large droplet steatosis" EXACT []
synonym: "macrovesicular steatosis" EXACT []
