[Term]
id: MP:0031356
name: abnormal outer dense fiber morphology
def: "any structural anomaly of the highly condensed, modified intermediate cytoskeletal filaments strengthened by disulfide linked keratin proteins that progress from the midpiece to the principal piece of the sperm tail; outer dense fibers are believed to maintain flagellar elasticity and play a role in the protection of the sperm tail against shear forces during epididymal transport and ejaculation" [https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsif.2018.0668, PMID:29168316]
comment: In the mammalian sperm tail, each ODF, which is numbered from 1 to 9 according to differences in cross-sectional shape and size, connects with one microtubular doublet of the axoneme resulting in a 9 + 9 + 2 cross-sectional pattern. In the principal piece, the fibrous sheath replaces ODFs 3 and 8, and the sizes of other ODFs progressively decrease distally. The shapes, sizes and lengths of ODFs are quite distinct in different species. {xref="PMID:29168316"}
synonym: "abnormal ODF morphology" EXACT []
synonym: "abnormal outer dense fibre morphology" EXACT []
is_a: MP:0008892 ! abnormal sperm flagellum morphology
is_a: MP:0020378 ! abnormal cell cytoskeleton morphology
property_value: dc:date "2022-02-14T12:57:48Z" xsd:dateTime
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6490-7723

