[Term]
id: MP:0031745
name: T wave inversion
def: "a downward (negative) deflection of the T wave in ECG leads where it is typically upright; the T-wave is considered negative if its terminal portion is below the isoelectric baseline, regardless of whether its other parts are above the baseline" [HP:0010872, https://ekgecho.de/the-t-wave-physiology-variants-and-ecg-features/, https://litfl.com/t-wave-ecg-library/]
comment: Normal repolarization of the ventricles typically produces upright T waves in most leads. While isolated T-wave inversions (TWIs) can be normal, particularly in specific leads, the presence of TWI in two or more anatomically contiguous leads is generally considered pathological. Any new T-wave inversion compared with prior ECGs is deemed abnormal.
synonym: "inverted T wave" EXACT []
synonym: "inverted T waves" EXACT []
synonym: "inverted T-wave" EXACT []
synonym: "negative T wave" EXACT []
synonym: "negative T-wave" EXACT []
synonym: "T-wave inversion" EXACT []
synonym: "TWI" EXACT []
is_a: MP:0010505 ! abnormal T wave
